# What NOT to upload when deploying the webapp.
#
# This matters more than it looks. When a .vercelignore exists the CLI stops
# falling back to .gitignore, so anything not listed here is uploaded even if
# git ignores it. That is how a deploy came to carry gpu-server/ - 50 GB of
# model weights, COGs and job results - and fail with "Request Entity Too
# Large" before it ever reached the build.
#
# The site needs: src/, public/, index.html, package.json, vite.config.js,
# vercel.json and api/ (api/media/[name].js is a serverless function - do not
# add it here). Everything else is server-side or local tooling.

# ── heavy server-side trees: none of this is part of the web build ──
gpu-server/
ml/
local-server/
height-server/
urbanly-node/
qgis-plugin/
keepalive-worker/
backend/
datasets/
docs/

# ── build output and dependencies: Vercel installs and builds remotely ──
node_modules/
dist/
.venv/
__pycache__/
# The CLI reads .vercel/project.json off disk to know which project this is;
# listing it here only keeps its 18 MB of cached build output out of the
# upload, it does not unlink the project.
.vercel/

# ── bundles and logs ──
*.zip
*.log
build_out*.txt
build_output.txt
error.txt

# ── data files that belong on the GPU server, not the CDN ──
*.gpkg
*.pth
*.pt

# NSV source video. NSV.jsx reads it from VITE_NSV_MEDIA_BASE (R2), proxied
# through api/media/[name].js - it has never been served from here, as a range
# request for /nsv/drone_web.mp4 returning text/html (the SPA fallback) shows.
# At 636 MB it was most of the failed upload.
*.mp4

# NOTE: don't blanket-ignore *.tif — the guided-tour sample chips live in
# public/tour/ and must ship. The large dataset .tif files are already
# excluded via backend/ and datasets/.
